  "result": "Neither five nor ten yet: more agents don't always give better results, so **start with one**.\n\nIt's tempting to think of agents as extra teammates, each adding skill. But every split creates handoffs, and an agent only knows what's in the text it's given.\n\nThink of *Vanderpump Rules*: news passes through three cast members, and key context is gone by the time it reaches the person it's about. Agents don't need to want drama for details to get lost (that's where the comparison breaks).\n\n```\nONE:    student → [assistant] → manuals, calendar, booking\n\nSPLIT:  student → [router] →handoff→ [manual agent]\n                           →handoff→ [booking agent]\n```\n\nExtra agents can also mean more model calls and text you pay for, which matters on your sponsor's budget. They're also more places to hunt for bugs.\n\nSplitting **can** pay off when one agent struggles with too many tools or too much material, or when big jobs can run separately in parallel. Test one agent on real student requests first. Split only if you see a failure that splitting would fix. That's also an argument you can defend at design review. A full lesson on this could come next if you like.\n\n**Your turn:** a teammate says, \"Booking sometimes picks the wrong date, so let's add five agents.\" What would you check first, and why?",
